findings.body:
**A command killed by an external signal is reported as a spawn failure.**

The contract at src/ops/dev/effects.ts:10-11 says that only a spawn failure rejects. The guard at Line 204-207 accepts only three cases: a numeric `code`, `error.killed === true`, or `ABORT_ERR`.

A child can also end from a signal that this runner did not send. Examples are the OOM killer's SIGKILL on a large vitest run, or SIGSEGV/SIGABRT from a crashing toolchain. In that case Node sets `code: null`, `killed: false`, and `signal: '<SIG>'`. The callback then goes to `mapSpawnFault` at Line 224, and the error text says `<command> could not be spawned`. `guardDevOp` turns this into `needs-human` with that text. The operator then looks for a missing binary, not a crashed gate.

The result still fails closed, so no gate turns green by mistake. Only the diagnosis is wrong. A command that ran and died from a signal should resolve as a verdict with `exitCode: null`, and callers then classify it as red.

<details>
<summary>Proposed fix</summary>

```diff
           const code = (error as { code?: unknown }).code;
+          const signalled = typeof (error as { signal?: unknown }).signal === 'string';
           if (
             !error.message.includes('maxBuffer') &&
-            (typeof code === 'number' || error.killed === true || code === 'ABORT_ERR')
+            (typeof code === 'number' || error.killed === true || code === 'ABORT_ERR' || signalled)
           ) {
```
</details>
